 "overview": {
  "summary": "The digital half of the Power Amplifier, on its own SIP plugged into the bottom of A16. An 82C55 programmable peripheral interface (U11) on the guarded digital bus generates 24 outputs: port A is a common data bus into two 5801 relay drivers, U10 for the four latching relays K1-K4 on A16 (lines LC0*-LC7*) and U12 for the non-latching relays K10-K17 and FETs Q57/Q58 (lines C0*-C7*), each driver strobed and enabled from port C. Port B goes through the two LM339 comparators U13 and U15 to make control lines SW0-SW7: SW0-SW2 feed the 74LS138 decoder U9, whose CONT0*-CONT7* outputs switch FETs and solid-state switches on A16; SW3 drives Q50/Q51; and SW4-SW7 leave through the motherboard to the Filter/PA Supply (A18) to set the +PA and -PA supplies to their +/-185 V or +/-365 V modes. A 4051 analog multiplexer (U14), addressed by PC0-PC3, selects one of eight diagnostic MUX signals from A16 onto the SDL line for the adc on the DAC assembly.",
  "sections": "§2-140, §2-66",
  "note": "Everything this board does is visible only through A16: its outputs drive A16's relays and switches, and its one input from the analog side is the eight MUX lines. Fault codes 3500 to 3503 exercise U11 directly; 3521 is the power-up 'is A16 responding' test that is really asking whether this SIP answers on the bus; 3530 catches a lost control word. The two 26-pin headers P1 and P2 are the whole interface, and reseating the SIP is the first step for any of those codes."
 },
